This document describes an approach applied to packaging cURL for build2. In
particular, this understanding will be useful when upgrading to a new upstream
version.

The upstream package contains the libcurl library and the curl program that we
package separately (see the respective README-DEV files for details). It also
contains tests that we currently don't package.

We add the upstream package as a git submodule and symlink the required files
and subdirectories into the build2 package subdirectories. Then, when required,
we "overlay" the upstream with our own source files, placing them into the
library directory.

Note that symlinking upstream submodule subdirectories into a build2 package
subdirectory results in creating intermediate build files (.d, .o, etc) inside
upstream directory while building the package in source tree. That's why we
need to make sure that packages do not share upstream source files via
subdirectory symlinks, not to also share the related intermediate files. If
several packages need to compile the same upstream source file, then only one
of them can symlink it via the parent directory while others must symlink it
directly. We also add the `ignore = untracked` configuration option into
.gitmodules to make sure that git ignores the intermediate build files under
upstream/ subdirectory.

The upstream package can be configured to contain a specific feature set. We
reproduce the union of features configured for the upstream source package in
Debian and Fedora distributions. The configuration options defining these sets
are specified in the Debian's rules and Fedora's RPM .spec files. These files
can be obtained as follows:

$ wget http://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/c/curl/curl_8.23.0-1.debian.tar.xz
$ tar xf curl_8.23.0-1.debian.tar.xz

$ wget https://kojipkgs.fedoraproject.org/packages/curl/8.23.0/1.fc46/src/curl-8.23.0-1.fc46.src.rpm
$ rpm2cpio curl-8.23.0-1.fc46.src.rpm | cpio -civ '*.spec'

As a side note, on Debian and Fedora the source, library, headers, and tools
are packaged as follows:

                src   library   headers               tool
Debian/Ubuntu:  curl  libcurl4  libcurl4-openssl-dev  curl
Fedora/RHEL:    curl  libcurl   libcurl-devel         curl

Search for the Debian and Fedora packages at https://packages.debian.org/search
and https://src.fedoraproject.org/.

Here are the discovered configuration options.

Debian:

  --disable-dependency-tracking --enable-symbol-hiding
  --enable-versioned-symbols --enable-threaded-resolver --with-lber-lib=lber
  --with-gssapi=/usr --with-nghttp2 --with-ngtcp2 --with-nghttp3
  --with-zsh-functions-dir=/usr/share/zsh/vendor-completions
  --with-fish-functions-dir=/usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d
  --without-libssh --with-libssh2
  --with-openssl --with-gnutls
  --with-ca-path=/etc/ssl/certs
  --with-ca-bundle=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt

Fedora (the full build):

  --disable-manual --disable-static --enable-hsts --enable-ipv6
  --enable-symbol-hiding --enable-threaded-resolver --with-gssapi
  --with-libidn2 --with-nghttp2 --with-ssl
  --with-ca-bundle=/etc/pki/ca-trust/extracted/pem/tls-ca-bundle.pem
  --with-zsh-functions-dir --enable-dict --enable-gopher --enable-imap
  --enable-ldap --enable-ldaps --enable-mqtt --enable-ntlm --enable-pop3
  --enable-rtsp --enable-smb --enable-smtp --enable-telnet --enable-tftp
  --enable-tls-srp --enable-websockets --with-brotli --with-libpsl
  --with-libssh --with-zstd --with-nghttp3 --with-ngtcp2

Note that upstream has dropped SMB and TLS-SRP support and so the configure
script ignores the --enable-smb and --enable-tls-srp options.

The union of these feature sets translates into the following options:

  --enable-symbol-hiding --enable-versioned-symbols
  --enable-threaded-resolver --with-lber-lib=lber --with-gssapi --with-libssh2
  --with-nghttp2 --with-ngtcp2 --with-nghttp3 --with-zsh-functions-dir=<path>
  --with-fish-functions-dir=<path> --with-ca-path=<path>
  --with-ca-bundle=<path> --enable-ipv6 --with-openssl --enable-ldap
  --enable-ldaps --with-brotli --with-zstd --with-libidn2
  --with-libpsl --with-libssh --with-gnutls
  --enable-hsts --enable-dict --enable-gopher --enable-imap
  --enable-mqtt --enable-ntlm --enable-pop3 --enable-rtsp
  --enable-smtp --enable-telnet --enable-tftp --enable-websockets

We, however, drop the external dependencies that are not packaged for build2,
disable default CA bundle/directory and use --with-ca-fallback instead,
explicitly request to use zlib, brotli, and zstd, and end up with the
following options:

  --enable-symbol-hiding --enable-versioned-symbols
  --enable-threaded-resolver --enable-ipv6 --with-openssl --with-zlib
  --with-brotli --with-zstd --disable-ldap --disable-ldaps --disable-ares
  --disable-manual --without-lber-lib --without-gssapi --without-libssh
  --without-libssh2 --without-nghttp2 --without-ngtcp2 --without-nghttp3
  --without-zsh-functions-dir --without-fish-functions-dir
  --without-libidn2 --without-libpsl --without-libgsasl --without-rustls
  --without-gnutls --without-ca-bundle --without-ca-path --with-ca-fallback
  --enable-hsts --enable-dict --enable-gopher --enable-imap --enable-mqtt
  --enable-ntlm --enable-pop3 --enable-rtsp --enable-smtp --enable-telnet
  --enable-tftp --enable-websockets

See the configuration options description at the "Install from source" page
(https://curl.se/docs/install.html).

On Windows we also enable the SSL backend provided by the system and make it
a default one. For MinGW GCC we add the following configuration options:

  --with-schannel --with-default-ssl-backend=schannel

For MSVC we pass the following options to CMake (see below):

  -DCURL_USE_SCHANNEL=ON -DCURL_DEFAULT_SSL_BACKEND=schannel

Upstream has dropped the Secure Transport backend and now offers the Apple
SecTrust certificate verification for the OpenSSL backend instead. Note that
it is only used for the native CA store (CURLSSLOPT_NATIVE_CA, etc), so its
enabling doesn't change the default behavior. On MacOS we add the following
configuration option:

  --with-apple-sectrust

Note, though, that lib/vtls/apple.c includes <Security/Security.h> which fails
to compile with GCC (see curl issue 4821 for details). Thus, we disable it
for GCC on MacOS (see libcurl/curl_config.h for details).

Normally, when packaging a project, we need to replace some auto-generated
headers with our own implementations and deduce compilation/linking options.
For cURL we can rely for that on configure.ac, m4/curl-compilers.m4,
{lib,src}/Makefile.am, and CMakeLists.txt. In practice, however, that can be
uneasy and error prone, so you may also need to see the auto-generated files
and the actual compiler and linker command lines in the build log. If that's
the case, you can configure/build the upstream package on the platform of
interest running the following commands in the upstream project root
directory.

On POSIX and for MinGW GCC (add --with-apple-sectrust for MacOS and
--with-schannel --with-default-ssl-backend=schannel for MinGW GCC):

$ autoreconf -fi
$ mkdir build
$ cd build
$ ../configure --enable-symbol-hiding --enable-versioned-symbols \
  --enable-threaded-resolver --enable-ipv6 --with-openssl --with-zlib \
  --with-brotli --with-zstd --disable-ldap --disable-ldaps --disable-ares \
  --disable-manual --without-lber-lib --without-gssapi --without-libssh \
  --without-libssh2 --without-nghttp2 --without-ngtcp2 --without-nghttp3 \
  --without-zsh-functions-dir --without-fish-functions-dir \
  --without-libidn2 --without-libpsl --without-libgsasl --without-rustls \
  --without-gnutls --without-ca-bundle --without-ca-path --with-ca-fallback \
  --enable-hsts --enable-dict --enable-gopher --enable-imap --enable-mqtt \
  --enable-ntlm --enable-pop3 --enable-rtsp --enable-smtp --enable-telnet \
  --enable-tftp --enable-websockets \
  >build.log 2>&1
$ make V=1 >>build.log 2>&1

For MSVC (upstream has dropped the winbuild/ makefiles and so we use CMake):

> cmake -B build -DBUILD_STATIC_LIBS=OFF -DCURL_USE_OPENSSL=ON ^
    -DCURL_USE_SCHANNEL=ON -DCURL_DEFAULT_SSL_BACKEND=schannel ^
    -DCURL_CA_BUNDLE=none -DCURL_CA_PATH=none -DCURL_CA_FALLBACK=ON ^
    -DCURL_DISABLE_OPENSSL_AUTO_LOAD_CONFIG=ON -DCURL_ZLIB=ON ^
    -DCURL_BROTLI=ON -DCURL_ZSTD=ON -DCURL_DISABLE_LDAP=ON ^
    -DUSE_NGHTTP2=OFF -DUSE_LIBIDN2=OFF -DCURL_USE_LIBPSL=OFF ^
    -DCURL_USE_LIBSSH2=OFF -DENABLE_CURL_MANUAL=OFF >build.log 2>&1
> cmake --build build --verbose >>build.log 2>&1

See the upstream/docs/INSTALL.md and upstream/docs/INSTALL-CMAKE.md for
details.

When the packaging is complete, build all the project packages in source tree
and make sure that no cURL headers are included from the system, running the
following command from the project root:

$ fgrep -a -e /usr/include/curl `find . -type f -name '*.d'`
